y only organization in the  initial shutdown that was going out and  checking on folks and so people didn't  even know what was going on  yeah they didn't know what's going on  they didn't know how to access services  and now that things have opened  certain agencies aren't even fully  opened for appointments social security  for example  so if people have identities stolen  which you need  to get into a place to rent you can't  get that now because you can't just  say walk in or if you go to the dmv what  we've been doing um to help our clients  is we get in line at like 4 30 or 5 in  the morning  so we can get in the building to get  their id  that day to hold their hands sort of  almost through the process well they  they meet us at eight because that's  when they open and then they get in  because there are no appointments right  now at the dmv until later on in the  fall  and so yeah what does one do if you  can't get your id and you can't access  those resources  you're just stuck in this never-ending  loop of homelessness if you have no idea  you can't get a job  bank situations whatever the case may be  you know even during the pandemic there  were efforts made the city the county  and other  organizations to expand where there was  the  the um they set up that area right by  cashman field  uh content yeah the tent sort of area  there  um so it seemed like there were some  good faith efforts at that time  how much of that from what you're seeing  is following through  now is there still that push that that  effort  on a government level or on a non-profit  level by other organizations  to um to address this issue absolutely  and i do know  the tent that was a big debacle you know  that made worldwide press is kind of uh  and people mocked it but something that  people didn't know on the back end a lot  of what we got  was covid cares funding so it was basic  needs assistance funding through the  feds and that was a great surge into our  community  lots of organizations got it we got it  and we're able to expand through it  um but it we couldn't put it towards  permanent housing or permanent  structures so that was unfortunately a  federal hang-up  so even though all that money came  through then other programming we've had  to supplement  to keep it going or to create something  more permanent for our clients so that  has been the struggle  for the government entities okay now  what and so there have been other monies  like the marijuana funds to go into the  community that will help with that  and so i know the county and the city  are being proactive to try to figure out  okay  now what's the next step we got this  money we can't keep it our time just ran  out actually on the 30th of june so  we're all  now doing our reporting deadline but all  pencils down we can't spend any more  money  now what do we do seems like a lot of  red tape for something that has been an  issue for a long time in the city  absolutely absolutely in this community  as a whole and it's not just our  community  many other communities struggle with  this too is that you don't have  community buy-in  and the communities that successfully  and homelessness are where they have  buy-in  from the private sector and the faith  sector here we don't really see that  as much and so what we have is the  little bit  that our government can can give us from  the federal level on down  and so that just isn't ever going to be  enough like we still are always going to  have a gap  before the pandemic the city passed  some controversial ordinances which i  know you were involved in fighting  against  with regards to camping on the streets  uh issues of homelessness sleeping on  sidewalks and so forth  they passed the impression i got from  talking with folks at city hall at least  is that  you know the pandemic overshadowed a lot  of that and their priorities were  elsewhere what is happening on the  ground from that level are those rules  actually still being enforced are you  speaking with folks that live on the  street that are that are homeless  that are dealing with this on a somewhat  regular basis  and what are their concerns we are and  what we're seeing is it's getting harder  for us to find people the  handful of people that we have that are  literally street homeless that were case  managing  um they'll get rousted or they'll get  moved or they'll get you know threatened  with citations and so then they move  to areas and so then we can't find them  or they get arrested and we can't find  them so it's really hard to get things  done when people are in jail and  unfortunately this is another  red tape loophole thing that we have  going on with the feds if someone is in  jail over 45 days they get taken off the  community housing list  so you basically by throwing people in  jail then are disqualifying them for  housing if it's for an extended jail  stay  so it's it's really hard it's it's yeah  well  you you know you know when i've spoken  with whether it's uh law enforcement  city officials the impression i've  gotten is that they aren't really  enforcing it they're  threatening to enforce it um is that the  in your opinion anyway the best way to  go about this in terms of trying to  motivate people who are living on the  street who are who don't have a home or  whatever the case may be  to get assistance i always say we've  failed  if law enforcement is doing our housing  or threatening  to do you know you do this or else and  if all you have in your tool belt is a  hammer everything looks like a nail so  they are law enforcement they are doing  what they are told to be they are not  uh homeless service providers i say this  is  it's not working we can see it's not  working if you look out on the streets  anybody can see it's not working  if you threaten people they're just  going to run away that's not a good  option so as homeless providers i also  own this we failed too because you know  if we're here  th