s and what that is and how  that helps sure  i always want everybody to come but so  i'm really excited  and i'm so thankful clark county was  administering the grant they picked us  to get uh more funds so we are expanding  so we currently have a farm operation  it's an indoor hydroponic farm anybody  can come down and take a peek at it if  you want just let us know  but it's at 11th in fremont so at the  back of the bunkhouse lot or behind  11th street records right across from  the ferguson's downtown they're off 11th  in fremont  and so we grow produce half of it is for  anthem medicaid members living within a  two mile radius because that's a food  desert  they get free produce anthem tracks are  overall health outcomes and metrics  the other half we sell for sale to the  bars and the restaurants so your mint  your basil any of those craft cocktails  that you're getting at any of those  fremont east bars  we've probably grown some of that  produce for that and so that covers the  cost of who we hire we hire formerly  homeless  right now we're serving veterans with  u.s vets so we hire them it's a jobs  reintegration program and then we have  on wednesday's wellness wednesday  programming so we have heart math  meditation  we have trauma recovery yoga and we have  workouts with our partners the lv bar  stars  i joke that it's like disneyland people  that work for us but we know our client  base and this is where i think  programming has gone wrong  we've catered it to them we asked them  what do you want  what do you need we listen to them and  we literally change it each month we  have a team meeting where we sit with  them we say what can we do better  what's not working what do you want to  do and that's how we go from there and  so that's i think the success in our  sauces  we just ask and we listen and and when  when you talk to them  i guess as a final question what do they  say they need  the most what is there aside from just a  place to live which is understandable  but  what is the biggest complaint the  biggest concern they have on a  day-to-day basis  i think it would blow everybody's mind  but i think the viewers need to hear  this  when we did our surveys at the end of  the last semester with our practicum  student  the number one thing they needed was  community  they were lonely people in the streets  are lonely  people indoors are lonely what have we  done with our society  that we are so focused inwardly we're  individualized you know  we're on our phones we're segregated  isolated and now we're dehumanized now  we're other rising  and i think not only do we need these  great programs and the government we  need it's a combo right  it's kind of like that that rubik's cube  of what you need  or tetris i guess it would be tetris to  put it all together to work  so it's you need the housing you need  the supportive services you need the  support of  the government and the private  private citizens and you need community  to surround them and help them  interesting meredith thanks for coming  in appreciate it thank you thank you for  having me

